The code statements that lead to the bug are:
static void epf_ntb_epc_cleanup(struct epf_ntb *ntb)
{
    epf_ntb_mw_bar_clear(ntb, ntb->num_mws);
    epf_ntb_db_bar_clear(ntb);
    epf_ntb_config_sspad_bar_clear(ntb);
}
Reason:
The `cmd_handler` delayed work is not stopped before the cleanup function clears BAR mappings, doorbells, and configuration space. This allows the work handler `epf_ntb_cmd_handler` to run after resources are freed, causing a use-after-free when it accesses those freed regions, leading to a kernel paging request crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
static void epf_ntb_epc_cleanup(struct epf_ntb *ntb)
{
    disable_delayed_work_sync(&ntb->cmd_handler);
    epf_ntb_mw_bar_clear(ntb, ntb->num_mws);
    epf_ntb_db_bar_clear(ntb);
    epf_ntb_config_sspad_bar_clear(ntb);
}
Reason:
Adding `disable_delayed_work_sync(&ntb->cmd_handler)` ensures that any pending or executing work is cancelled and waits for completion before tearing down resources. This prevents the work handler from accessing freed memory, eliminating the use-after-free and the associated crash.

